{
  "id": "can-cant",
  "level": "a1",
  "area": "Here and now",
  "title": "can and can't",
  "tagline": "One little word for ability, permission and polite requests.",
  "cefr": "A1",
  "exam": [
    "Speaking Part 2 — talking about abilities and free time",
    "Listening Part 4 — a conversation about likes and abilities"
  ],
  "why": "<i>Can</i> is one of the most useful words in English: with it you say what you're able to do, ask for things politely and give or ask for permission. Examiners love asking <i>What can you do?</i> And <i>can</i> never changes — no -s, no <i>to</i> — which makes it one of the easiest verbs once you know the rule.",
  "hook": {
    "text": "Sports day at Nordic! \"<b>Can</b> you swim, Liam?\" asks Miss Vega. \"Yes, I <b>can</b> — but I <b>can't</b> run fast,\" says Liam. \"<b>Can I</b> try the long jump instead?\" \"Of course you can!\"",
    "ask": "Find can and can't in the story. Which sentence talks about something Liam is good at, and which about something he finds hard?"
  },
